Still no crash detected. The issue is that the overflow writes into the `_buf` field of the same struct, which is within the same allocation. ASan doesn't detect intra-object overflows.

Let me think about this differently. The OSS-Fuzz crash was detected by ASan. This means the overflow must be writing past the end of the entire struct, not just past the `_packet` field. Let me check if the struct layout is different in the version that was fuzzed.

Actually, looking at the struct definition again:
```c
struct message {
    unsigned short int id;
    struct { ... } header;
    unsigned short int qdcount;
    unsigned short int ancount;
    unsigned short int nscount;
    unsigned short int arcount;
    struct question *qd;
    struct resource *an;
    struct resource *ns;
    struct resource *ar;
    unsigned char _packet[MAX_PACKET_LEN];
    unsigned char *_buf;
    unsigned char *_bufEnd;
    unsigned long _len;
    unsigned long _label;
    char *_labels[MAX_NUM_LABELS];
};
```

The `_packet` field is at some offset, and `_buf`, `_bufEnd`, etc. come after it. Writing past `_packet` writes into these fields. But if `_packet` is the last field (or if the fields after it are moved before it in a different version), the overflow would write past the end of the struct.

